1
00:04:04,137 --> 00:04:10,137
They had nothing to lose, they risked it all.

2
00:08:08,275 --> 00:08:14,275
The Commitments is a comedy, drama, music film directed by Alan Parker.

3
00:12:12,413 --> 00:12:18,413
Jimmy Rabbitte, just a thick-ya out of school, gets a brilliant idea: to put a
soul band together in Barrytown, his slum home in north Dublin.

4
00:16:16,551 --> 00:16:22,551
First he needs musicians and singers: things slowly start to click when he finds three fine-voiced females virtually in his back
yard, a lead singer (Deco) at a wedding, and, responding to his ad, an aging trumpet player, Joey "The Lips" Fagan.

5
00:20:20,689 --> 00:20:26,689
Jimmy Rabbitte, played by Robert Arkins.

6
00:24:24,827 --> 00:24:30,827
Steven Clifford, played by Michael Aherne.

7
00:28:28,965 --> 00:28:34,965
Imelda Quirke, played by Angeline Ball.

8
00:32:33,103 --> 00:32:39,103
Natalie Murphy, played by Maria Doyle Kennedy.

9
00:36:37,241 --> 00:36:43,241
Mickah Wallace, played by Dave Finnegan.

10
00:40:41,379 --> 00:40:47,379
Bernie McGloughlin, played by Bronagh Gallagher.

11
00:44:45,517 --> 00:44:51,517
The Commitments is a 1991 musical comedy-drama film
based on the 1987 novel by Roddy Doyle.

12
00:48:49,655 --> 00:48:55,655
It was directed by Alan Parker from a screenplay written
by Doyle, Dick Clement and Ian La Frenais.

13
00:52:53,793 --> 00:52:59,793
Set in the Northside of Dublin, the film tells the story of Jimmy Rabbitte (Robert Arkins), a young music
fanatic who assembles a group of working-class youths to form a soul band named "The Commitments".

14
00:56:57,931 --> 00:57:03,931
The film is the first in a series known as The Barrytown Trilogy,
followed by The Snapper (1993) and The Van (1996).

15
01:01:02,068 --> 01:01:08,068
Producers Lynda Myles and Roger Randall-Cutler acquired the film rights to the novel in 1988,
and commissioned Doyle, a first-time screenwriter, to write an adaptation.

16
01:05:06,206 --> 01:05:12,206
Doyle spent one year working on the script before Myles brought in veteran
screenwriters Clement and La Frenais to help complete it.

17
01:09:10,344 --> 01:09:16,344
Upon reading the novel, Parker signed on as the film's director in 1989.

18
01:13:14,482 --> 01:13:20,482
An international co-production between Ireland, the United States and the United Kingdom, The Commitments was
the first film produced by Beacon Pictures, which provided an estimated budget of $12–15 million.

19
01:17:18,620 --> 01:17:24,620
The film's young lead actors were mostly inexperienced, and were cast because of their
musical backgrounds and resemblance to the characters in the novel.

20
01:21:22,758 --> 01:21:28,758
Principal photography took place in Dublin, from late August to October, 1990.

21
01:25:26,896 --> 01:25:32,896
The Commitments underperformed at the North American box office,
grossing $14.9 million during its theatrical run.

22
01:29:31,034 --> 01:29:37,034
Reviewers praised the music, performances and humour, while criticism was
occasionally aimed at the pacing and Parker's direction.

23
01:33:35,172 --> 01:33:41,172
The film resulted in two soundtrack albums released by MCA Records; the first reached number eight on the Billboard
200 album chart and achieved triple-platinum status, while the second album achieved gold sales status.

24
01:37:39,310 --> 01:37:45,310
At the 1992 British Academy Film Awards, the film won four of six BAFTA Awards for
Best Film, Best Direction, Best Adapted Screenplay and Best Editing.

25
01:41:43,448 --> 01:41:49,448
It also received an Academy Award nomination for Best Film Editing.

26
01:45:47,586 --> 01:45:53,586
The film has since gained cult status.

27
01:49:51,724 --> 01:49:57,724
The film explores themes of dublin, ireland, ireland,
soul music, band, the barrytown trilogy.

28
01:53:55,862 --> 01:54:01,862
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
